{{smaller block|{{right|''Burns'', A Mother’s Lament.}}}}

'''With young''', pregnant; gravid.
{{smaller block|So many days my ewes have been ''with young''.
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., ii. 5. 35.}}}}

'''Young of the year''', in ''ornith.'', specifically, birds which have left the nest and acquired their first plumage. Most birds hatch in summer, and, after putting off the down-feathers characteristic of the nestling, acquire a special first feathering; and as long as this is worn, or until the first true molt, they are ''young of the year'', without regard to the length of time this plumage may be worn, as it is always replaced by the following spring.

'''younger†''' (yung′gėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yonger'', ''ʒonger'', ''ʒungre'', ''ʒingre'', etc., < AS. ''gyngra'', ''gingra'', ''gengra'' (= G. ''jünger'', etc.), a follower, disciple, lit. a younger person (as distinguished from ''yldra'', an elder), compar. of ''geong'', ''giung'', ''iung'', young: see ''young''.] A young person; a disciple. ''Shak.'', M. of V., ii. 6. 14 (quartos).

'''youngerly''' (yung′gėr-li), ''a.'' [< ''younger'', compar. of ''young'', + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>, after ''elderly''.] Somewhat young; below middle age. [Colloq., U. S.]
{{smaller block|The life-blood of Christendom flows in the veins of her ''youngerly'' men.{{right|''Church Union'', Jan. 11, 1868.}}}}

'''young-eyed''' (yung′īd), ''a.'' Having the fresh, bright eyes or look of youth.
{{smaller block|Still quiring to the ''young-eyed'' cherubins.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., v. 1. 62.}}}}

'''youngheadt†''' (yung′hed), ''n.'' [< ME. ''yonghede''; < ''young'' + ''-head''.] Youth.
{{smaller block|<poem>Elde was paynted after this,
That shorter was a fote, iwys,
Than she was wont in her ''yonghede''.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 351.}}}}

