'''4.''' Same as ''junker''.

'''youpon''' (yö′pọn), ''n.'' Same as ''yapon.''

'''your''' (yör), ''pron.'' [(''a'') < ME. ''your, ȝour, ȝoure, ȝure, iour, eower,'' < AS. ''eówer'' (= OS. ''iuwar'' = OHG. ''iuwer'' = Goth. ''izwara''), gen. of ''gē'' (dat. acc. ''eów''), you: see ''ye''<sup>1</sup>, ''you.'' (''b'') < ME. ''your, ȝour, ȝoure, ȝowre, iour, ower, our, eoure, eowre, eower,'' < AS. ''eówer'' = OS. ''iuwar, iuwa'' = OFries. ''iuwe,'' etc., = Goth. ''izwar,'' poss. pron.: see (''a''), above.] '''A†.''' ''pers. pron.'' Of you: the original genitive of ''ye''<sup>1</sup>, ''you.''
{{smaller block|Sitthen I am ''ȝoure'' alre hefd [i. e., head of you all],<br>ich am ''ȝoure'' alre hele [salvation].
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xxii. 473.}}}}

'''B.''' ''poss. pron.'' '''1†.''' Of you; belonging to you: used predicatively: now replaced by ''yours.''
{{smaller block|I wolde permute [change] my penaunce with ''ȝowre.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xiii. 110.}}
I . . . mot ben ''youre'' whil that my lyf may dure.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parliament of Fowls, l. 642.}}
And she ansuerde, “I am ''youre'' and the childe ''youre'', therfore do with me and with hym youre will.”
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 89.}}}}

'''2.''' Belonging to you: possessive and adjective in use, preceding the noun. {{smaller|While plural in form and original meaning, it is now commonly also used, like the nominative ''you'', in addressing an individual.}}
{{smaller block|“I haue no kynde knowyng,” quod I, “to conceyue alle ''ȝoure'' wordes.” {{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), viii. 57.}}
Promise unto the Lord ''your'' God, and keep it, all ye that are round about him.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Psalter, Ps. lxxvi. 11.}}}}

