¶
{{smaller block|I leave it [the poem] to ''your'' honourable survey, and ''your'' honour to ''your'' heart’s content.
{{right|''Shak.'', Venus and Adonis, Ded. to the Earl of Southampton.}}}}
{{smaller block|[''Your'' was used formerly to denote a class or species well known. This use survives as an archaism, and now often adds a slurring or humorous significance.

''Your'' serpent of Egypt is bred now of ''your'' mud by the operation of ''your'' sun.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 7. 29.}}

''Your'' great Philosophers have been voluntarily poor.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 352.]}}}}

'''yourn''' (yörn), ''pron.'' Yours. [Prov. Eng. and U. S.]

'''yours''' (yörz), ''pron.'' [< ME. ''youres'', ''ȝoures'', etc.; with added poss. suffix, as in ''ours'', ''theirs'', etc.: see ''your''.] That which belongs or those which belong to you: the possessive used without a following noun. {{smaller|Preceded by ''of'', it is equivalent to the personal pronoun ''you'': as, a friend ''of yours''. Compare the similar phrases made with the other possessives in the independent form.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye cruell one! what glory can be got
In slaying him that would live gladly ''yours!''</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Sonnets, lvii.}}

What’s mine is ''yours'' and what is ''yours'' is mine.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. for M., v. 1. 543.}}

''Yours'' is no love, Faith and Religion fly it.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Wife for a Month, i. 1.}}

If by Fate ''yours'' only must be Empire, then of necessitie ours among the rest must be subjection.
{{right|''Milton'', Hist. Eng., ii.}}

[''Yours'' is sometimes used in specific senses without reference to a noun previously mentioned: (''a'') Your property. (''b'') The persons belonging to you; your friends or relatives.}}

