{{smaller block|<poem>Bothe to me & to myne mykull vnright,
And to yow & also ''yours'' ȝomeryng [mourning] for euer.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1722.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O God, I fear thy justice will take hold
On me, and you, and mine, and ''yours'' for this!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., ii. 1. 132.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Your letter: as, ''yours'' of the 16th inst. is at hand.}}
{{smaller block|I have ''yours'' just now of the 19th.
{{right|''Swift'', To Dr. Sheridan, July 27, 1726.]}}}}

Abbreviated ''yrs.''

'''Yours truly, yours to command''', etc., {{smaller|phrases of conventional politeness immediately preceding the signature at the end of a letter: hence sometimes used playfully by a speaker in alluding to himself.}}
{{smaller block|''Yours truly'', sir, has an eye for a fine woman and a fine horse. {{right|''W. Collins'', Armadale, II. 168. (''Hoppe.'')}}}}

'''yourself, yourselves''' (yör-self′, -selvz′), ''pron.'' [< ME. ''your selven'', etc.: see ''your'' and ''self''.] An emphatic or reflexive form of the second personal pronoun, ''ye, you.'' {{smaller|''Yourself'' is used when a single person is addressed (compare ''ye, your''), and ''yourselves'' when more than one. As nominatives, the words are used for emphasis, either in apposition with ''you'' or alone.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye se well ''your-seluyn'' the sothe at your egh,
Hit is no bote here to byde for baret with-oute.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 12333.}}}}
{{smaller block|I knowe yow alle as wele or beter than ye do ''youre-self.''
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 141.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Conversation is but carving;
Carve for all, ''yourself'' is starving.</poem>
{{right|''Swift'', Verses on a Lady.}}}}
{{smaller|In the objective case ''yourself'' or ''yourselves'' is commonly reflexive: when emphatic it is usually in apposition with ''you.'' Compare ''himself, herself'', etc.}}
{{smaller block|Call forth your actors by the scroll. Masters, spread ''yourselves.'' {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., i. 2. 16.}}}}

